                    884.0
                ]
            ]
        },
        {
            "block_label": "text",
            "block_content": "From now on nodes are indicated in teletype face and classes of networks in bold face. Figure 4(a) shows an example of the top level network of an OOBN; it comprises three chance nodes (X1, X6, X9), and two instance nodes (or instances, represented as rounded rectangles) of classes  $ Instance\\ 1 $ and  $ Instance\\ 2 $ which are BNs themselves as shown in Figure 4(c) and 4(d) respectively. Consider for example 4(c). X1 and X5 are input and output nodes, graphically characterized by an outer grey ring with a dashed line and a full line respectively. In detail X1 imports information from the top level network (Figure 4(a)) into  $ Instance\\ 1 $ and X5 exports information from  $ Instance\\ 1 $ to the top level network (Figure 4(a)). Figure 4(b) summarises the entire OOBN and reflects its hierarchical nature. In fact, it provides a picture of the top level network with expanded instances thus showing both the chance nodes and the interface (input and output) nodes of the two instances.",
            "block_bbox": [
                95,
                885,
                994,
                1089
            ],
            "block_id": 6,
            "block_order": 6,
            "group_id": 6,
            "block_polygon_points": [
                [
                    95.0,
                    885.0
                ],
                [
                    994.0,
                    885.0
                ],
                [
                    994.0,
                    1089.0
                ],
                [
                    95.0,
                    1089.0
                ]
            ]
        },
        {
            "block_label": "text",
            "block_content": "As for arrows, their meaning may differ from that of statistical dependence when at least one endpoint is not an ordinary chance node. In particular, arrows from chance nodes to input nodes are identity links. So in Figure 4(b) the arrow from the chance node X1 to the input node X1 of Instance 1 represents the fact that information about variable X1 is transferred identically within Instance 1. Differently, arrows from output nodes may be either standard statistical dependence links pointing to a chance node, or functional links pointing to a function node associated with an expression depending on its parents.",
            "block_bbox": [
                95,
                1091,
                994,
                1227
            ],
            "block_id": 7,
            "block_order": 7,
            "group_id": 7,
            "block_polygon_points": [
                [
                    95.0,
